# Repository map

These repositories are separate checkouts, not submodules. A session that needs
more than one must be granted access to the others; paths are configured locally.

| Repo             | Owns                                             | Depends on                       |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
| `<hub-repo>`     | Cross-cutting plans, the merged data store       | `<vocab-repo>`, `<mirror-repo>`  |
| `<vocab-repo>`   | The curated vocabulary                           | -                                |
| `<toolkit-repo>` | Reusable code shared by the others               | -                                |
| `<mirror-repo>`  | Mirrored upstream metadata                       | -                                |

<!-- Confirm which of these are actual git repos vs. plain working folders. It
     changes what worktrees, checkpoints, and per-repo auto memory can do. -->

## Which one is authoritative for what

State this explicitly - it's the question that costs the most time when it's
ambiguous.

- **Vocabulary**: `<vocab-repo>` is the source of truth. Do not define new
  terms in `<hub-repo>`; propose them for `<vocab-repo>` instead.
- **Mirrored metadata**: `<mirror-repo>` mirrors its upstream. Treat it as
  read-only downstream; never hand-edit mirrored records.
- **The merged store**: `<hub-repo>` owns it. Any separate working area is
  staging only.
- **Reusable code**: `<toolkit-repo>`. If a helper is being written twice, it
  belongs there.

## Retired / do not extend

- `<retired-integration>` - superseded by `<its-replacement>`. Do not add
  features to it. The reasoning is in the hub's `.status/decisions.md`.

